WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.070
Voice-over Hank: Good morning, John.

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:05.320
So, I went to France last week, where I was
presenting at a ridiculously fancy advertising

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:10.709
industry convention and I spent most of my
week alternating between just being tremendously

00:00:10.709 --> 00:00:16.299
satisfied at how pleasant everything was and
being powerfully disturbed by ostentatious

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:17.480
displays of wealth.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.480
A friend of mine had a friend who had a yacht
and they were like "You should come and see

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:23.501
this yacht" and I was like "Dude, I've seen
boats before" and they were like "No, this

00:00:23.501 --> 00:00:24.501
is not a boat."

00:00:24.501 --> 00:00:28.770
And so I went and indeed, upon arrival on
the boat I immediately wanted to see the engine

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:33.920
room and the water treatment and desalination
systems, the yacht was very cool, and very

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:34.920
pretty.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:39.460
It has a crew of 12 people who live and work
on the yacht and there's a 24 hour diner and

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:43.800
it costs $250,000 to fill up the gas tank
of the yacht.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.680
Not voice-over Hank: It seems like a nice
place to have a party.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.360
Voice-over Hank: So as far as "Thoughts from
places" places go, the South of France is

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.940
a place that is very easy to have some thoughts
from.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:52.940
Not voice-over Hank: Dog!

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:57.890
Voice-over Hank: One of the main goals of
advertising is to create need where no need

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:03.490
exists, and I had a hard time not thinking
about that as I wandered around and was constantly

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:08.890
besieged by an AT&amp;T AdWorks campaign that
excitedly implied the unquestionable benefit

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:09.890
of more.

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:14.170
In a place that rich people spend lots of
money to go to so they can spend lots of money

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:19.060
at fancy shops and restaurants, manufactured
discontent is both everywhere and invisible.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:21.229
Not voice-over Hank: The air conditioner's
hitting my toes!

00:01:21.229 --> 00:01:22.450
You gotta insulate better.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:26.020
Voice-over Hank: This is a place where people
go to realize how much they don't have.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:30.079
Even if you can afford to spend 2,000 euro
on a jacket, there's a store around the corner

00:01:30.079 --> 00:01:32.270
with a 9,000 euro jacket.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:36.399
Even if you can afford a really big boat,
there's a bigger boat next door.

00:01:36.399 --> 00:01:40.380
And particularly at this event, not only is
there always something you can't afford, but

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:45.320
there's always a better party with cooler
people that you're not invited to.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:49.890
I was surprised not that I was a little upset
by all of this, but why I was a little upset.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:54.530
It wasn't just because of the tremendous difference
that this wealth could make in the lives of

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:59.520
people who are suffering, but also because
the monstrous displays of wealth I saw seemed

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.060
to come from sad places, like, mostly from
personal insecurity.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:07.399
People want other people to know that they
are important and that they matter and that

00:02:07.399 --> 00:02:08.830
they're powerful and cool.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:13.370
John, we're headed into a world of such tremendous
productivity that there is hope in me that

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:18.700
someday we will have so many resources that
everyone will be able to minimize their suffering,

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:23.000
but when I see that new world taking shape
in places like Cannes and Saint-Tropez, I

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.090
worry that no one will ever be satisfied with
what they have, and so wealth will never stop

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:33.360
concentrating and we won't be able to turn
our surpluses to truly global goals like ridding

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.780
ourselves of poverty and war and the global
insecurity caused by climate change.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:42.620
Of course, at the same time all around I saw
families who loved each other and were enjoying

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:46.680
the beauty and wonder of this absolutely gorgeous
and historically fascinating place.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:50.630
It reminds me that many of the smartest people
in the world aren't famous.

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:54.280
They're people who are smart enough to realize
that real happiness doesn't come from being

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:58.260
the biggest fish with the most people tripping
over themselves to be like them.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:04.230
It comes from being personally secure in yourself
and your life and your values and your future.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:07.440
That has something to do with money, but definitely
not everything.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.770
It also has to do with being comfortable with
the life and with the identity that you've

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:15.680
created for yourself and doing things you
love with people you love.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:18.950
Not voice-over Hank: Hey Katherine.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:20.260
Katherine: What?

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:21.560
Hank: Mediterranean.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:22.560
Mediterra-knee-out.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.880
Katherine: Wow.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:28.020
Hank: Mediterra-knee-in.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:29.020
Mediterra-knee-out.

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:31.160
Katherine: (laughs) Congratulations.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:35.160
Voice-over Hank: John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:36.630
